nd me to email my  mom and that experience is way better  than going in to the reminders app and  doing this no matter how beautiful the  reminders app is it will never be as  easy as me just saying do it another one  is home automation here's an example of  me turning lights on and off with an  well actually here's the app that you do  it with so here's me turning lights on  and off with the app very nice and now  here's me doing it  Alexa turn off the lights in the master  bedroom now watch this okay Alexa can  you turn on the bedroom turn on the  master bedroom lights and that is  natural language processing right I  completely botched that command seven  different ways and it still was able to  pick it up and do it you know what's  even better is if you have a nest  thermostat you can lay in bed and  control the temperature with your voice  and now that I've said that sounds  really pathetic you know what never mind  I don't ever do that the last example  I'd like to give you as a service called  digit digit is a remarkable little  service that saves for you using machine  learning based on your spending patterns  and it will give you savings updates I  blurred this out so you don't get too  depressed by my low balance and the  whole interface is conversational so it  has an app now but before all you did  was chat with it to save to withdraw and  it was pretty resilient at understanding  my commands I didn't have to know what  to say I just said it and I thought that  that was quite remarkable so the  question now is is UI as we know it dead  the answer is no no absolutely not there  are accessibility concerns you wouldn't  want to activate take photos with your  voice you wouldn't want to scroll  through pictures with your voice right  like scroll up scroll down scroll that  that's not a good user experience but it  does give us a chance to rethink some of  the ways that we build UI is that maybe  we don't need to build a UI and instead  what we need to build 